---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
---

<Base
  title="Readme"
  description="Ben Mayer's Readme — a user guide to my values and how I work."
>
  <div class="container readme-page">
    <section class="hero">
      <h1>Ben.Readme</h1>
      <p>A user guide on me and how I work. It captures my guiding values, how I work as a human, and will hopefully help you understand me better and help us work together.</p>
    </section>

    <div class="prose readme-body">
      <h2>North Star Ideals</h2>
      <h3>Do good</h3>
      <p>
        Doing good is one of my strongest motivators. Sometimes that means
        positive impact, sometimes it means work I'm proud of, sometimes it's
        just something that feels meaningful to me. When what I do lines up
        with what I value, I do my best work.
      </p>

      <h3>Keep growing</h3>
      <p>
        Growth is important to me, regardless of how large or small. If I'm
        doing the same thing for too long without learning something new, I
        get bored fast. Growth comes from making mistakes and picking
        yourself up again. Growth needs honesty and self-reflection: owning
        mistakes, acknowledging flaws, and understanding I don't know it all.
      </p>

      <h3>Enjoy the moment together</h3>
      <p>
        It's too easy to speed through life, chasing the next thing, the next
        goal. Pause from time to time and practice gratitude for what you've
        achieved. Celebrate and laugh together, it's what makes it all
        worthwhile. Life's short.
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>

      <p>
        <strong>I thrive on being challenged,</strong> and I like to
        challenge myself. Being challenged helps me understand my own
        thinking and quickly exposes flaws in my logic or gaps in my
        analysis. It's also how I find out I don't fully believe in an
        initiative: if I can't confidently defend it, something's missing. I
        naturally apply the same probing to others, because it's the fastest
        way for me to get to the bottom of something. I know it can come
        across as distrust, or as if I think your ideas aren't good. That's
        not the case at all. If this style doesn't work for you, let me know
        and I'll adapt.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>I feel very comfortable with ambiguity,</strong> and I enjoy
        making sense of chaos. I naturally think in outcomes, constantly
        asking: What's the purpose? What are we trying to achieve? How does
        this fit the bigger picture? Solving puzzles is one of my biggest
        intrinsic motivators. What I've learnt is that the environments I
        find comfortable can be stressful for others. Not having a plan
        months ahead feels fine to me, because I deeply trust our ability to
        figure it out. If structure gives you security, let's build it
        together.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>Weirdly enough, I kind of like some pressure.</strong> It
        makes me focus on what really matters and strips out what isn't
        essential. When shit hits the proverbial fan, I find it easy to stay
        calm, block out the turbulence and find a practical way forward. I'm
        also aware the pressure that sharpens me can drain others, so I keep
        an eye on how the team is holding up.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>I value open, transparent communication.</strong> I've spent
        a lot of time figuring out how and where I work best, and I'm happy
        to adjust my ways if it helps you do the same. That only works if
        we're honest with each other about what's working and what isn't. It
        also means I like to know where I stand, so I can focus on the work
        instead of analysing how I'm perceived. The same goes the other way:
        if I come across too strong (I've been told I can be intimidating),
        tell me and I'll dial it down. And fair warning, I pull the angriest
        face when I'm concentrating, something like this 😠. On video calls
        it can look like I'm furious at what you're saying. I'm not. Call me
        out and you'll get a big 😃
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>I believe in trust and ownership, not control.</strong>
        That's how I lead teams, how I treat peers, and how I like to be
        treated. You don't have to earn my trust, you have it from day one. I
        assume you love what you do and want to do a good job, same as me. In
        return, I expect ownership, and I'll hold you to the same standards I
        hold myself to. If something's off, I'll tell you early and directly.
        I also know not everyone wants full autonomy on day one, and that's
        fine: more frequent check-ins are always an option.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>I'm comfortable working async.</strong> I'm used to
        collaborating across locations and time zones, so I lean on clear
        written updates, docs and recorded walkthroughs. Live face-to-face
        time is invaluable though, best used for debates, and getting to
        know people.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .readme-page {
    max-width: 42rem;
    padding: 0 1.5rem 4rem;
  }

  .readme-page .hero {
    text-align: center;
    padding: 4rem 0 2rem;
  }

  .readme-body h3 {
    margin-top: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }
</style>
